.TH LIKEOS-GPT 1 "November 2026" "LikeOS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
likeos-gpt \- show, check and edit GUID partition tables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B print
.I device
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B verify
.I device
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B info
.I device n
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B new-table
.I device
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B add
.I device
.RB [ \-n
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-start
.IR pos ]
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-\-type
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-uuid
.IR guid ]
.RB [ \-\-attrs
.IR hex ]
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B delete
.I device n
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B resize
.I device n
.IR size | max
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.BR name | type | attrs
.I device n value
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.BR repair | wipe
.I device
.br
.B likeos-gpt
.RI [ options ]
.B types
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B likeos-gpt
reads and writes the GUID partition table (GPT) of a disk: the protective
MBR in the first sector, the primary table (header and partition entries)
after it, and the backup copy at the end of the disk.
It is the tool of the LikeOS installer's partitioning library (libgpt) for
the command line: everything the installer does to a partition table can be
looked at and done by hand with it.
.PP
.I device
is a block device
.RB ( /dev/sda ,
.BR /dev/nvme0n1 )
or a disk image file.
Only the editing commands write, and only to the device named.
After writing to a block device
.B likeos-gpt
asks the kernel to read the new table again, so the partition devices
.RB ( /dev/sda1 ,
.BR /dev/nvme0n1p1 ...)
follow at once; a disk with a mounted partition may refuse that, and the
change is then seen after the next start.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI print " device"
Show the table: the disk's size, sector size and GUID, the usable sectors,
and one line per partition with its number, first and last sector, size,
type and name.
.TP
.BI verify " device"
Check both copies of the table: the header and entry checksums, the
positions the headers give, overlapping or out-of-range partitions, and
whether the two copies agree.
Problems found give exit status 1.
.TP
.BI info " device n"
Show partition
.I n
in detail: its type (name, code and GUID), its unique partition GUID, the
first and last sector, the size, the name and the attribute bits.
.TP
.BI new-table " device"
Write a new, empty GPT with a protective MBR.
A device that already has partitions is refused unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.TP
.BI add " device"
Create a partition, by default in the first free region, starting at the
first 1 MiB boundary in it and filling the rest of it.
The add options below choose otherwise.
.TP
.BI delete " device n"
Delete partition
.IR n .
The data on the disk is not touched.
.TP
.BI resize " device n size" \fR|\fPmax
Move the end of partition
.IR n :
.I size
is its new size,
.B max
makes it reach the next partition or the end of the usable sectors.
The start stays where it is, and the file system in the partition is not
changed: grow the file system after growing the partition (for ext4
.BR resize2fs (8)),
shrink it before shrinking the partition.
.TP
.BI name " device n name"
Set the name of partition
.I n
(UTF-8, at most 36 UTF-16 code units).
.TP
.BI type " device n type"
Set the type of partition
.IR n .
.TP
.BI attrs " device n hex"
Set the 64 attribute bits of partition
.IR n ,
as a hexadecimal number (bit 0: required by the platform, bit 1: ignored by
the firmware, bit 2: legacy BIOS bootable, bits 48\(en63: defined by the
partition type).
.TP
.BI repair " device"
Rebuild a damaged copy of the table from the intact one, move a backup copy
that is not at the end of the disk (a disk image that was made larger, a
disk copied to a bigger one) to the end, and rewrite a missing or wrong
protective MBR.
When both copies are intact but differ, the primary one is kept.
.TP
.BI wipe " device"
Erase the partition table: both GPT copies and the MBR.
Needs
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B types
List the partition types
.B likeos-gpt
knows by name: alias, four-digit code, GUID and description.
.SH ADD OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-number " \fIn\fR"
The partition number (default: the first unused one).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-start " \fIpos\fR"
The first sector (default: the start of the first free region, aligned to
1 MiB).
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-size " \fIsize\fR"
The size (default: the rest of the free region it starts in).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " \fItype\fR"
The partition type (default:
.BR data ).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-name " \fIname\fR"
The partition name.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-uuid " \fIguid\fR"
The unique partition GUID (default: a random one).
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-attrs " \fIhex\fR"
The attribute bits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output (JSON) of
.BR print ,
.BR verify ,
.B info
and
.BR types ,
and of the partition
.B add
created.
.TP
.BR \-\-sector\-size " 512|4096"
The logical sector size of a disk image file (block devices report their
own).
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Confirm a command that destroys a partition table:
.B new-table
over an existing table, and
.BR wipe .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reread
Do not ask the kernel to read the table again after writing it.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary of the commands and options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH SIZES, POSITIONS AND TYPES
A position or size that is a plain number counts sectors.
With a suffix it counts bytes:
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.B T
(or
.BR KiB ,
.BR MiB ,
.BR GiB ,
.BR TiB )
are powers of 1024, so
.B 512M
is 512 MiB.
.PP
A
.I type
is an alias, a four-digit code or a GUID.
The common ones:
.PP
.RS
.nf
esp           ef00  EFI system partition
likeos\-root   8304  LikeOS root file system
data          8300  generic data
swap          8200  swap
bios\-boot     ef02  BIOS boot
home          8302  home directories
basic         0700  basic data (NTFS, exFAT, FAT)
msr           0c01  reserved (MSR)
recovery      2700  recovery environment
lvm, raid     8e00, fd00
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B likeos-gpt types
lists them all.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed, or
.B verify
found problems.
.TP
.B 2
Wrong usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Partition a disk the way the installer does for a whole-disk installation:
.PP
.RS
.nf
likeos\-gpt \-\-yes new\-table /dev/sdb
likeos\-gpt add /dev/sdb \-\-size 512M \-\-type esp \e
        \-\-name "EFI system partition"
likeos\-gpt add /dev/sdb \-\-type likeos\-root \-\-name likeos\-root
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a disk image and repair it after it was made larger:
.PP
.RS
.nf
likeos\-gpt verify disk.img
likeos\-gpt repair disk.img
.fi
.RE
